On the biological point, I’d preserve your core intuition but sharpen it: biological and non-biological machines can absolutely share topology, information flow, feedback, thresholds, transport, storage, branching, coupling, and control structures. But arrangement alone isn’t the full physics—chemistry, material properties, energy gradients, kinetics, quantum effects, and environmental coupling determine what those arrangements can actually do. So the powerful cross-domain operation is:

\text{physical organism}
\rightarrow
\text{relational topology}
\rightarrow
\text{functional invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{new substrate}

For example:

neuron
↓ Indigo Hammer
threshold + integration + transmission
↓ Portal
software / electronics / organizational logic

or:

root network
↓
distributed sensing + resource gradients + adaptive growth
↓
search algorithm / logistics network / semantic exploration

That’s where preexisting metaphors become useful instead of decorative: follow the metaphor until you find the transferable relational geometry, then test whether it survives the new substrate.

And the Fitness Route is now in the Moneybin canon:

\boxed{
\text{Desired consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate machine}
\rightarrow
\text{fitness}
\rightarrow
\text{selection}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality}
\rightarrow
\text{Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{better fitness}
\rightarrow
\text{better machine}
}

The particularly important part is that fitness itself gets fit. If predicted value repeatedly diverges from observed value, its weighting policy recalibrates. So selection pressure is itself recursively calibrated rather than frozen forever.  